Ladybird serializes the selected rendered text. Blink’s
DomSelection::toString updates layout and passes the visible
range to PlainText with unselectable content excluded.
WebKit’s DOMSelection::toString reads the frame selection.
Gecko’s Selection::Stringify passes SkipInvisibleContent
to its document encoder.

Chrome 158.0.8127.37, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 all omitted script,
style, and display:none text. Chrome and Firefox inserted two newlines
between two zero-margin paragraphs. WebKit inserted one. Chrome and
WebKit applied text-transform and omitted image alternative text.
Firefox included the alternative text. All three kept a space between
words across a soft wrap.

When a selection contains only a preserved line feed, Ladybird keeps
it, as Firefox and WebKit do. Chrome and WebKit also keep the space
before a preserved line feed between two spans when text follows it.

All three engines omit a trailing separator when selecting the first
cell or row. Blink checks ShouldEmitTabBeforeNode and WebKit checks
shouldEmitTabBeforeNode. Gecko tracks the cells already serialized in
nsPlainTextSerializer::DoOpenContainer with mHasWrittenCellsForRow.

Ladybird excludes unselectable cells and rows from the selected text,
but still visits descendants that allow selection. Chrome and Firefox
omit the text of a table with user-select:none and preserve text from
selectable descendants. WebKit includes all the cells in that table.

Ladybird includes SVG text and tspans in selected text. Blink reads the
selected DOM offsets through GetMappingUnitsForDOMRange in
TextIteratorTextNodeHandler. WebKit reads the text renderer in
TextIterator::handleTextNode. Gecko serializes visible text through
Selection::Stringify and nsPlainTextSerializer::AppendText.

Chrome 158.0.8127.37, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 all included
visible SVG text in full and partial selections. All three included a
visible tspan inside a hidden text element. Firefox and WebKit preserved
the spaces on both sides of a line feed when a selection covered one
text node under white-space:pre-line. Chrome removed both spaces.

Ladybird keeps a selected line feed when the nearest caret boundary
skips an adjoining collapsible space. All three browsers kept the line
feed when two spans split the text on either side of it. Chrome and
WebKit also put separators around visible SVG text embedded between
two HTML text nodes. Firefox joined the surrounding text directly.

Ladybird omits separators for wholly hidden table rows and cells and
keeps both tabs around a hidden middle cell. Chrome 158.0.8127.37 and
WebKit 26.5 omitted wholly hidden rows. Firefox 151.0 included their
separators. All three kept both tabs around a hidden middle cell.
